+# Philosophy
+
+For whoever proposes adding a service to larder, or weighs two good options that no record settles. It holds what the
+team favors. Written after issue 70 in October 2026 proposed Elasticsearch for item search: the third proposal to add
+a service, argued again from the start, each one settled by the same rule.
+
+## Tenets
+
+### A new service joins only when Postgres cannot do its job and two volunteers can restore it
+
+Larder runs one Postgres database and one web app, with its worker, and the volunteers who run it are few. Each
+service added is one more thing someone must restore on a Saturday night. Its history:
+
+- In 2023 Redis was proposed for sessions. It was turned down because only one volunteer would have run it, and
+  sessions went to Postgres.
+- Redis did run Celery's queue until March 2025, when it evicted 300 receipt jobs one Saturday night. One volunteer
+  was the only person who could restore it. Jobs moved to Postgres.
+- In October 2026 Elasticsearch was proposed for pantry item search, because searching for "rice" took four seconds
+  and missed "Rice, brown". Postgres full-text search on the item name brought it to 40 ms and found "Rice, brown",
+  so the proposal took that path instead.
+
+This rule turns down a service that does what Postgres already does, and a service that only one volunteer could
+restore. It still allows a service outside larder that nobody on the team runs: the card gateway, or a hosted
+service. Applied in [keep sessions in the database](adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) and
+[run background jobs on Postgres](adrs/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md).
